ligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{4}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{5}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186430361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186430362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186430363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186430364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1045 "Singular Value Decomposition of Larger Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186430361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430259 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7a17cc21-6ccb-5439-ab56-b058df3482c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430260 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430260 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430260 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08e2fd28-e984-57d1-8558-2dee685815ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430261 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430261 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430261 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10847312-dde2-55cc-bbce-32b3f2b8723c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430262 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430262 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430262 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "112c77b9-4e4a-5aa1-98f4-ea988ca16426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430263 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430263 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186430263 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11a82eac-0835-5adf-a299-eda8